# Signatures for the mixed-program corpus, in the declaration format
# `zaqaru --signatures` reads.
#
# Milestone 2 of the interop plan supplies every boundary signature this way,
# on purpose: it settles the marshalling before inference exists to feed it,
# so that a later failure is a failure of inference rather than of the thunk
# it fed. Milestones 3 and 4 delete these in two halves and require the same
# corpus to keep passing.
#
# Pointers are `i32` because the target is wasm32, where an address really is
# 32 bits wide.

# --- the transpiled half, called from foreign wasm or from the test ---
#
# Inference recovers all of these from the machine code, so they are not
# declared. The one exception below is not a shortcoming of the analysis but a
# property of the convention: SysV fills the integer and floating-point
# argument registers independently, so `f(i32, f64)` and `f(f64, i32)` produce
# byte-identical assignments and nothing records which order the source used.
# Inference refuses rather than guessing — a wrong signature at a boundary is
# worse than none, because none keeps the uniform wrapper, which always works
# — and a declaration is how the source gets to say what only it knew.
#
# It also keeps the override path exercised: declarations must beat inference,
# and a test that never declares anything would not notice if they stopped.
guest_uses_mixed(i32, f64, f32, i64) -> i32

# --- the foreign half, reached through generated thunks ---
#
# Nothing is declared here either. The wasm object being linked against states
# the type of every function it defines, and `--thunks` reads it: that is
# knowledge rather than inference, and it is the only thing that can recover a
# function whose arguments are all passed straight through, since a caller
# that never touches an argument register leaves no trace of it.
#
# Call-site inference is the fallback for a foreign function no object
# defines — a host import — and is graded on its own in tests/inference.rs.
